    <node id="吴国">
      <data key="d0">吴国</data>
      <data key="d1">地点</data>
      <data key="d2">Wu State (吴国) was an ancient Chinese kingdom that played a significant role during the Spring and Autumn Period (770–476 BCE). It was ruled by notable kings such as Wu Wang Helü (吴王阖庐) and Wu Wang Fuchai (吴王夫差), with Fuchai being one of its most prominent rulers. The state was characterized by its longstanding rivalry and enmity with Yue State (越国), which was considered its sworn enemy. This hostility defined much of Wu's political and military activities, culminating in numerous conflicts and wars between the two states.

Wu State was also the homeland of renowned historical figures such as Wu Zixu (伍子胥), a loyal minister and strategist who played a key role in its governance and military campaigns. The state was known for its skilled swordsmen and warriors, contributing to its reputation as a militarily capable kingdom. Wu served as a significant setting for various historical and legendary events, reflecting its importance in the cultural and political landscape of the era.

Despite its power and influence, Wu State ultimately fell to Yue State, marking the end of its prominence in Chinese history. Its legacy, however, continues to be remembered through historical records and stories that highlight its rulers, conflicts, and contributions to ancient Chinese civilization.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-2a72fd2ce03a23a58c65d98d2258f3e1&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-bb8a415d6907f0cafb86835c412854f7&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c6f4631a6bc7286dd9560f06b9845d9e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9f20b87683fe0cc8608a321ff18feb71&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-42c3e318eb3b9f5e9c1bd450e13ff5b9&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-3b2a680eed881fd86d938031d895d21e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-939cc8f0980bb7b81255939246f7fe66&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e93cef7b7f368309937f9f274b9ad084&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f89960ee186e8b69358a7a5c384a3ee9&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9f3e590b3ab7f94ee3770d3739196401</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1777541096</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
